From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm: TF_GLM_OVN_RESTORE_HELD, a restore leaves the rows its sequence already holds

A warm turn restores its kept prompt state into a sequence and, a pass later, keeps the state again. Each is a copy
of the whole state between the sequence and the snapshot pages: on three GB10s at TP=3 an nsys capture of a ~113k
conversation shows 850 MiB in 10.8 ms for the restore and 858 MiB in 10.3 ms for the keep on rank 0, on every turn,
and the state is 7.5 MiB a 1,000 rows, so the cost grows with the conversation. Most of the restore's bytes are
already where it puts them: a sequence only appends rows, State.reset clears the KDA records and the conv rows and
nothing else, and a released slot keeps its sequence (slot 0 always, every slot under TF_GLM_OVN_KEEP_SEQS), so the
sequence that served the last turn still holds every latent row, scale and whole pool below the kept state's
position.

With TF_GLM_OVN_RESTORE_HELD=1 a restore into such a sequence copies only what a sequence rewrites: the arena head
(KDA records, conv rows, a window's saved rows), the index rings, each layer's pool in progress and spare row, the
head's caches and the tail. The rows left alone are the ones cuda_disk_image already stores by position
(Plan.byPosition names them from the live sequence; a host test compares the two counts).

How a sequence is known to hold a state. State.held is set to the state's id and position when the sequence keeps a
state (keepAs: the snapshot is a copy of the live rows) and when a restore into it completes. State.ready, which
every forward calls on each sequence before it writes rows at the sequence's position (a prompt chunk, a solo or
paired step, a batched pass, a verify window, a shared one), clears it when that position is below the mark's, and
a restore clears it before it copies, so a restore that fails midway leaves no mark. A restore of the state
the mark names (its id says which state, and so which position) skips the by-position ranges; any other restore is whole, as is every restore into a paged
sequence (Plan.byPosition names no range for one: its rows are on its pages, and a state that left pages to the
shared store is only kept by such a sequence). Each rank keeps its own mark and takes the same decision, since
every rank runs the same keeps, restores and forwards.

Which slot a request takes. Lanes.claim gave a stream the lowest free slot; with the switch a stream that resumes a
kept state takes the free slot whose sequence still holds it (Lanes.heldSlot), so two conversations that alternate
keep finding their own rows. A paged engine keeps the lowest free slot.

Default off: with the switch unset every restore copies every byte and claim picks the lowest free slot. Replies are
unchanged by construction (the bytes after the restore are the kept state's either way); the checks below decide it.

Host tests (cuda_restore_held_test.zig, on a driver whose device memory is host memory): eachBut's pieces; the
by-position ranges against cuda_disk_image.parts' rows by position, fp8 and bf16, at seven positions; a keep, every
other byte of the arena changed, then a restore: the bytes copied are the plan's less the by-position ranges and every
planned byte is the kept one; a planted control (a held byte changed is not repaired, and is once a forward below the
position clears the mark); the mark after a restore, after a keep of another state (one position on, and the same
position with other rows), and with the switch off; a restore
that fails midway leaves no mark and the next one is whole; a paged sequence names no range.
cuda_lanes_test.zig: the slot choice. GPU check: tf-cuda-test glm-restore-held-check on glm-synth's checkpoint (one
GPU): the sequence that kept a state resumes it, another sequence resumes it whole, a third runs the request cold;
every planned byte of the first two is equal and twelve greedy steps (tokens, logits rows, MTP drafts) are equal
across the three, at three keep positions (on the grid, inside a pool, where the index rings are nearly full), fp8
and bf16 caches, with a planted control each (a held byte changed before the resume stays changed with the switch and
is repaired without it). Not run on a GPU yet.

New here, not a re-seat: written on this stack at v1.2.9.

